2026-10-29 07:45:58 [nitro@ryzen5] what is overton window?
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.6 kimi-k3.1-pro 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.006 1.093KB 21.18s
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] The Overton window is the range of ideas or policies considered socially and
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] politically acceptable at a particular time. Ideas inside the window can be debated seriously;
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] ideas outside it are viewed as extreme, radical, or unthinkable.
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] It is named after Joseph Overton, who argued that politicians generally support only
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] policies they believe voters will accept. A common scale is:
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] • Unthinkable → Radical → Acceptable → Sensible → Popular → Policy*
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] The window can move or widen over time because of social movements, media coverage,
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] advocacy, crises, or changing public values. For example, an idea initially dismissed as radical
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] may gradually become mainstream and eventually be enacted into law. Ideas can also move in the
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] opposite direction.
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] The Overton window is about public acceptability, not whether an idea is morally right,
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] effective, or already legal. It can also differ between countries, communities, and political
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] groups. Sometimes people introduce more extreme proposals partly to make other proposals seem
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] moderate by comparison, though shifts in the window are not always deliberate.
2026-10-29 07:46:20 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
