2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.6 kimi-k3.1-pro 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.005 4.511KB 21.38s
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] No. “All rights reserved” means the copyright holder retains the rights granted by
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] copyright law. A license then grants others specific permissions—such as copying, modifying, and
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] redistributing—subject to its terms.
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] So the header effectively means:
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 	imon Armstrong retains copyright; everyone else may use the work under the Apache 2.0 license.
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] The phrase is largely redundant in modern copyright law, but it remains conventional. It would
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] conflict only with a dedication that surrendered copyright entirely, not with an ordinary
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] open-source license. If your module uses a license other than Apache 2.0, the header should name
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] that license instead.
2026-10-29 08:12:37 [kimi-k3.1-pro] 
