2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] last night exploring two major themes: the mechanics of Dependent Type Theory in Lean and a
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] conceptual coordinate transformation from spatial geometry to prime-factor arithmetic.
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] Here is my reading of your experiments and learnings from the session:
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] 1. Learning Lean: Proofs as Data ("Correctness by Construction")
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] In standard programming, you usually define a data structure (like a pair of numbers) and write a
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] separate function to validate whether those numbers follow the rules.
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] Your key Lean learning in `PrimePowerAbacus` was discovering how to bake the rules directly into
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] the type definition using inductive types and dependent types:
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] • Bounding the Domain: Instead of using raw natural numbers (`ℕ`), you used `Fin (W + 1)`
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] to ensure the inputs p, k, q, m are inherently bounded by the width W.
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] • Carrying the Proof: Your constructors `step_u` and `step_v` don't just take numbers; they
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] demand proofs (`Nat.Prime p.val` and `p.val ^ k.val ≤ W`) before an instance of `PrimePowerAbacus`
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] can even exist.
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] • Tactic Automation: In your `example` blocks, you learned how to satisfy these proof
