2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] 2. The Mathematical Experiment: From Circles to Prime Space
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] Your README outlines an intuitive chain of reductions aimed at taming the "jagged edges" of a
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] circle on a grid:
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] 1. Geometric to Linear: You started with the circle boundary (x² + y² \le R) and imagined
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] flattening the squares into a linear boundary (a + b \le R) in a single positive quadrant—turning a
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] curve into a uniform staircase (triangular numbers).
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] 2. Additive to Multiplicative: You then shifted from an additive constraint (a + b \le R)
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] to a multiplicative hyperbolic constraint (u · v \le W). In number theory, counting points under
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] the hyperbola u · v \le W is the classic Dirichlet Divisor Problem—which is deeply
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] analogous to Gauss's Circle Problem! Both problems are famous for having smooth areas with chaotic,
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] jagged error terms along their boundaries.
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] 3. The Abacus Concept: Because u · v \le W is governed by multiplication, the grid points
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] are no longer stepped through by +1, but are built out of prime powers (p^k and q^m). You
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] conceptualized an "abacus" where the axes (u and v) are traversed via prime-power states rather
2026-10-29 08:34:43 [gemini-4-argon] than simple linear increments.
